  <h2>What counts as an external document under ISO 9001?</h2>
  <p>
    An external document is any piece of documented information your quality system relies on that someone outside your organisation wrote and controls. You did not author it, and you cannot change its content. You can only decide how you receive it, store it and keep it current inside your own system.
  </p>
  <p>The most common categories a quality manager deals with are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Published standards</strong> you work to, such as ISO 9001 itself, a national standard, or an industry code of practice.</li>
    <li><strong>Customer specifications, drawings and purchase-order terms</strong> that define what you must deliver.</li>
    <li><strong>Laws and regulations</strong> relevant to your product, sector or export markets.</li>
    <li><strong>Supplier certificates and statements</strong>: certificates of analysis (COA), certificates of conformance (COC), safety data sheets (SDS), halal or kosher certificates, and allergen or GMO statements.</li>
    <li><strong>Calibration certificates</strong> issued by an external laboratory for your measuring equipment.</li>
    <li><strong>Accreditation and certification body documents</strong>, such as your own ISO 9001 certificate from a <a href="https://www.sanas.co.za/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">SANAS</a>-accredited body.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Documents you write yourself, such as standard operating procedures, policies, work instructions and forms, are not external documents. Those fall under internal document control instead, which is a separate discipline from what's covered here.
  </p>

  <h2>What does ISO 9001 clause 7.5.3.2 require?</h2>
  <p>
    Clause 7.5.3 sets out the general controls your organisation must apply to all documented information. It must be available and suitable where and when it's needed, adequately protected, and properly managed for distribution, storage, version control and retention. Sub-clause 7.5.3.2 extends those same controls specifically to documents of external origin that your organisation has determined are necessary for planning and operating the quality system.
  </p>
  <p>
    In plain terms, once you decide a customer specification or a supplier's certificate matters to your quality system, you must identify it, control how it's distributed and used, and make sure nobody works from a version that's been superseded or has lapsed. The standard doesn't prescribe a specific tool for this. A register is the most practical way most SMEs meet it.
  </p>
  <p>
    External documents also connect to clause 8.4, control of externally provided processes, products and services, the clause that governs supplier management more broadly. A certificate you hold on a supplier is both an external document under 7.5.3.2 and evidence you're meeting 8.4's requirement to verify what a supplier provides. Our <a href="/guides/supplier-certificate-tracking">guide to tracking supplier certificates</a> covers that side of the process in more detail.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of this requires a particular piece of software. A small company with two suppliers can meet clause 7.5.3.2 with a well-kept spreadsheet and some discipline about who checks it and when. What the clause does require, whatever tool you use, is that the current version is the one people work from, that you can show when each document was last checked, and that nobody can point to an expired certificate and say no one owned it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Clause 7.5 keeps the same numbering and the same intent in both the 2015 and the 2026 editions of the standard, so this guidance applies whichever one your organisation is certified to. Certificates issued to ISO 9001:2015 stay valid during the transition to ISO 9001:2026, so you don't need to change how you control external documents just because the standard has a new edition.
  </p>

  <h2>What should an external document register include?</h2>
  <p>
    A register is the simplest tool for this: one row per document, naming what it is, who issued it, when it was issued, when it needs to be reviewed or renewed, who owns it, and where the current version is held. That's usually enough to answer every question an auditor will ask about a specific document in seconds rather than minutes.
  </p>
