  <h2>How do you decide when to review or replace an external document?</h2>
  <p>
    Use the expiry date the issuer gives you wherever one exists. Most supplier certificates, halal and kosher certifications, and calibration certificates carry an explicit date. Where no expiry date is printed on the document itself, such as most safety data sheets, set a periodic review date instead, commonly annually, so someone still checks it hasn't been superseded by a newer revision.
  </p>
  <p>
    Customer specifications and drawings change on their own schedule, usually tied to a revision number rather than a date. The trigger to review is a new revision landing in your inbox rather than a calendar reminder. Your own ISO 9001 certificate is reviewed on the cycle your certification body sets, typically an annual surveillance audit with recertification every three years.
  </p>
  <p>
    Regulations and published standards are the hardest to keep current, because nobody sends you a notification when one changes. You have to go looking. Assign a named owner to each regulation or standard you rely on, and set a routine review date, even if it's only once a year, so checking for an update is somebody's job rather than an accident.
  </p>

  <h2>What do auditors check about external document control?</h2>
  <p>
    Auditors testing clause 7.5.3.2 usually ask to see specific documents and trace them through your system, rather than reading a procedure. The most common checks are:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Can you produce the current version of a named external document, and show it's the one in use on the floor or in the file?</li>
    <li>Can you show that a superseded or expired version isn't still sitting in a shared folder someone might open by mistake?</li>
    <li>Do you have evidence, such as a register entry, a reminder or an approval, that someone reviewed the document before its expiry date?</li>
    <li>Is there a named owner for each document, so it's clear whose job it is to notice when something lapses?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The most common finding is a missing owner or a missing review date, which leaves a certificate to expire quietly until an audit or a customer complaint surfaces it.
  </p>

  <h2>What does Obel MS External Document Control automate?</h2>
  <p>
    Obel MS is built around ISO 9001 clause 7.5.3.2, and its External Document Control module removes the manual work of keeping a register like the one above up to date:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>AI Suggestions.</strong> Upload a certificate once and the document type, supplier, manufacturer, product and dates come back as suggestions you Accept, Edit or Dismiss before approving.</li>
    <li><strong>Auto-filing by Supplier, Manufacturer and Product.</strong> Approved documents land in the right folder on their own, built from the document's own metadata, with nobody dragging a file into a tree by hand.</li>
    <li><strong>Document Status computed for you.</strong> Every document shows as Active, Expiring soon, Expired or No expiry set, calculated from its own dates rather than tracked in a separate spreadsheet.</li>
    <li><strong>Expiry digest by email and Teams.</strong> Before anything lapses, the people who need to know get a digest, instead of finding out at an audit.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Obel MS itself is not "ISO 9001 certified". No software can be, since certification applies to an organisation's quality system, assessed by an accredited body. What Obel MS gives you is the document control structure clause 7.5.3.2 expects, built and maintained for you. See the full <a href="/iso-9001">ISO 9001 clause map</a> for how each part of the standard maps to the product, or check <a href="/pricing">pricing</a> to see what's included from the Starter plan.
  </p>
